    (1778251562711, "bolus: 20–80 mg IV (max 200 mg single dose in resistant cases) | continuous: 0.5–1 mg/kg/h (DOSE-CHF: equivalent outcomes to bolus) | furosemide 40 mg = bumetanide 1 mg = torsemide 20 mg | continuous infusion = more consistent natriuresis, less peak trough variability, possible lower ototoxicity risk\n→ PHARM KEY: Infuse bolus dose over 1–4 min (≤4 mg/min IV to avoid ototoxicity). Faster infusion rates = increased cochlear toxicity risk. Monitor: urine output hourly, K⁺, Mg²⁺, Na⁺ every 6–8h with aggressive diuresis.", "Furosemide ICU dosing: IV bolus, continuous infusion, dose equivalent..."),
    (1778251562713, "potassium (K⁺) — inhibition of NKCC2 → loss of K⁺ in collecting duct | magnesium (Mg²⁺) — dependent on Na⁺ gradient | hypokalemia → arrhythmias in cardiac patients; hypomagnesemia → K⁺ CANNOT BE CORRECTED regardless of replacement amount (Mg required for Na/K-ATPase function)\n→ PHARM KEY: The K+/Mg²⁺ connection: never just replace K⁺ without checking Mg²⁺. If Mg is low, check and replace first — K⁺ correction will fail until Mg is adequate.", "Furosemide always depletes ___ and ___ — nurse ALWAYS monitors..."),
    (1778251562715, "hypertonic saline creates osmotic gradient WITHOUT diuresis (mannitol → urinary loss of Na⁺/water) → can correct hyponatremia while reducing ICP | less rebound edema, safer in hypovolemia (mannitol dehydrates), corrects hyponatremia simultaneously | patient is hemodynamically unstable or hyponatremic\n→ PHARM KEY: 23.4% NaCl via central line for acute herniation (30 mL bolus). 3% NaCl for ongoing ICP management. Correct Na⁺ ≤10 mEq/L per 24h to prevent central pontine myelinolysis (osmotic demyelination).", "Hypertonic saline (3% NaCl) for increased ICP..."),
    (1778436692044, "FIRST: BLOOD CULTURES (2 sets from 2 different sites) — must be obtained BEFORE antibiotics to preserve the ability to identify the causative organism and guide de-escalation\n| Why cultures first: once antibiotics are in the blood, organisms begin dying → culture bottles yield false-negative results → 7–14 days of broad-spectrum empiric therapy instead of targeted therapy guided by sensitivity data. The 5-minute delay to draw cultures is clinically trivial compared to this consequence.\n| ANTIBIOTICS within 1 HOUR of sepsis recognition — broad-spectrum empiric coverage targeting the most likely pathogens based on suspected source:\n  - Unknown source/septic shock: vancomycin (MRSA) + piperacillin-tazobactam (gram-negatives including Pseudomonas) ± antifungal if immunocompromised or recent antibiotics\n  - Pneumonia (CAP): beta-lactam + macrolide/azithromycin or respiratory fluoroquinolone\n  - HAP/VAP: vancomycin + cefepime or piperacillin-tazobactam\n| Mortality increases approximately 7–10% per hour of antibiotic delay in septic shock — one of the most time-sensitive drug administrations in medicine\n→ CCRN KEY: Blood cultures before antibiotics is an ORDER, not a suggestion. The nurse who administers antibiotics before drawing cultures has eliminated the possibility of organism identification. Even 5–10 minutes of urgency should not reverse this order.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: Antibiotic de-escalation (narrowing coverage once cultures return with sensitivities) is a stewardship goal — it requires having culture data. Cultures drawn after antibiotics are negative 30–40% more often than pre-antibiotic cultures. The broad-spectrum empiric regimen costs more, has more side effects, and promotes resistance. Pre-antibiotic cultures make de-escalation possible.", "Septic Shock 1-Hour Bundle [Pos 1/2] — FIRST action before antibiotics..."),
    (1778436692047, "(1) BLOOD CULTURES (2 sets) — FIRST, before antibiotics; preserves organism identification for targeted therapy and de-escalation\n| (2) BROAD-SPECTRUM ANTIBIOTICS within 1 HOUR — empiric coverage based on suspected source; every hour of delay ↑ mortality ~7–10%\n| (3) 30 mL/kg IV CRYSTALLOID bolus — if hypotension (SBP &lt;90 mmHg) OR lactate ≥4 mmol/L; NS or LR acceptable; complete within 3h of identification; reassess fluid responsiveness after initial bolus (don't give more fluid if patient is not fluid-responsive)\n| (4) VASOPRESSORS if MAP &lt;65 mmHg persists despite initial fluid bolus — Norepinephrine is first-line vasopressor in septic shock (alpha-adrenergic → SVR ↑; beta-adrenergic → maintains CO); target MAP ≥65 mmHg\n| (5) REASSESS FLUID RESPONSIVENESS — PLR (passive leg raise with LVOT VTI ≥10% change = fluid responsive) OR echocardiography. Test: PLR — positive PLR → give more fluid; negative PLR → vasopressors/investigate cause\n→ CCRN KEY: The 1-hour bundle is the operationalized version of the \"Hour-1 Bundle\" from SSC 2018 guidelines. All five elements should be initiated simultaneously when possible. The nurse manages elements 1, 3, and 5 (cultures, fluid, and reassessment); the physician orders elements 2 and 4.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: Lactate ≥4 mmol/L is a critical threshold in sepsis — it indicates tissue hypoperfusion (anaerobic metabolism) even if blood pressure appears acceptable. Cryptic shock (lactate ≥4 with normal BP) carries similar mortality to overt septic shock and requires the same aggressive initial management. Repeat lactate at 2 hours: failure to clear by ≥10% is a poor prognostic sign.", "[Synthesis] Septic Shock 1-Hour Bundle all 5 elements..."),
    (1778480387928, "CaO₂ (arterial oxygen content) = (Hgb × 1.34 × SaO₂/100) + (0.003 × PaO₂)\n| Dissolved O₂ (0.003 × PaO₂) contributes only ~30 mL/min at PaO₂ 100 mmHg = &lt;3% of total DO₂. Hgb-bound O₂ carries >97%.\n| Normal DO₂ ≈ 950–1000 mL/min (CO 5 L/min × CaO₂ ~20 mL/dL × 10)\n| Adjusting Hgb slider: each 1 g/dL change in Hgb changes DO₂ by ~134 mL/min (1.34 × SaO₂/100 × CO × 10). A larger absolute effect than most interventions.\n→ CCRN KEY: The 1.34 constant = mL O₂ bound per gram of Hgb when fully saturated. This is why Hgb is the dominant term — it is the carrying capacity. Hgb 7 vs Hgb 14 at the same SaO₂ and CO = half the DO₂. SpO₂ looks identical in both patients.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: Supplemental O₂ is often the first intervention for 'low sats' — but raising PaO₂ from 100 → 200 mmHg only adds ~30 mL/min to DO₂ (dissolved term only). This is why hyperoxia adds negligible DO₂ benefit in patients with normal or near-normal SpO₂. The interventions that actually matter: ↑Hgb (transfusion), ↑CO (inotropes, afterload reduction), ↑SaO₂ from pathologically low levels.", "DO2 = CO x ___ x 10. Component contributing LEAST..."),
    (1778480387931, "Critical threshold: approximately 330 mL/min — below this, O₂ consumption can no longer be sustained by increased extraction (maximum extraction ratio ~70–75% reached). Tissues switch to anaerobic metabolism.\n| O₂ consumption becomes SUPPLY-DEPENDENT — VO₂ falls proportionally with DO₂ instead of being demand-determined. Each mL of O₂ delivery reduction causes additional O₂ debt.\n| With CO 3.5, Hgb 7.2, SaO₂ 96%: DO₂ ≈ 3.5 × (7.2 × 1.34 × 0.96) × 10 ≈ 324 mL/min — AT or BELOW the critical threshold. Despite 'acceptable' vital signs, this patient has minimal O₂ reserve.\n| Clinical marker: ELEVATED LACTATE (>2 mmol/L) confirms anaerobic metabolism. The cells produce lactate when they cannot meet ATP demand aerobically. Lactate >4 mmol/L = significant O₂ debt.\n→ CCRN KEY: Lactate CLEARANCE (not just single value) is the resuscitation endpoint: ≥10% clearance per 2 hours confirms DO₂ has been restored above the critical threshold. Failure to clear despite hemodynamically 'normalized' parameters = DO₂ still inadequate.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: ScvO₂ and lactate together describe the O₂ supply-demand relationship: ScvO₂ ↓ (tissues extracting more) → approaching critical threshold → lactate ↑ (anaerobic) → DO₂ below critical threshold. The progression from ScvO₂ 55% → 48% → lactate 3.2 → 5.8 is the trajectory of deterioration. Intervene at ScvO₂ drop, before lactate rises.", "Critical DO2 threshold ~___. Below this, O2 consumption becomes ___ dependent..."),
    (1778480387934, "Current DO₂ ≈ 3.2 × (6.8 × 1.34 × 0.97) × 10 ≈ 282 mL/min — BELOW critical threshold (330). Lactate 3.6 confirms active anaerobic metabolism.\n| Intervention A (transfuse Hgb → 9.5): DO₂ ≈ 3.2 × (9.5 × 1.34 × 0.97) × 10 ≈ 394 mL/min (+112 mL/min)\n| Intervention B (dobutamine CO → 4.8): DO₂ ≈ 4.8 × (6.8 × 1.34 × 0.97) × 10 ≈ 423 mL/min (+141 mL/min)\n| Dobutamine raises DO₂ more in the calculation — but NOT the only consideration:\n  Transfusion: immediate effect, corrects the true deficit (anemia), no ↑HR or myocardial stress, evidence-based threshold (Hgb &lt;7 for non-cardiac; &lt;8 for cardiac post-op)\n  Dobutamine: ↑HR → ↑myocardial O₂ demand → risk in post-MI or post-CABG; arrhythmia risk; ↑VO₂ partially offsets ↑DO₂ gain\n→ CCRN KEY: In post-op anemic patients: TRANSFUSE — it corrects the anatomical cause of low DO₂ without cardiac stress. Dobutamine treats pump inadequacy, not anemia. TRICC trial: Hgb threshold 7 for stable ICU (non-cardiac); 8 for cardiac/hemodynamically unstable. The post-op setting with anemia is a transfusion indication.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: The DO₂ equation reveals WHICH lever to pull, but clinical context determines which is SAFE. The equation is a decision support tool — not a pure optimization algorithm. Choosing the intervention that most safely restores DO₂ above 330 mL/min while minimizing VO₂ increase is the therapeutic goal.", "Post-op patient: Hgb 6.8, SaO2 97%, CO 3.2, lactate 3.6. DO2 = ?..."),
    (1778808560000, ">=10% reduction at 2h | >=20% reduction at 6h | The green curve on the chart shows adequate clearance crossing below 4 mmol/L (shock threshold) by ~4 hours; the red curve fails to clear, remaining in the shock range despite adequate MAP.\n→ CCRN KEY: Lactate clearance is superior to MAP alone as a resuscitation endpoint -- patients can have occult hypoperfusion with MAP >65. Clearance &lt;10% at 2h warrants reassessment of fluid responsiveness and source control.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: Type B lactic acidosis (liver failure, metformin toxicity, thiamine deficiency) impairs clearance without tissue hypoperfusion -- correlate with ScvO2 &gt;70% and clinical exam before escalating vasopressors.", "SSC 2021 targets lactate clearance ___ at 2h and ___ at 6h..."),
    (1778808560003, "~6.8% at 6h (target >=20%) | Persistent occult hypoperfusion -- inadequate resuscitation despite acceptable hemodynamic endpoints | Next: reassess fluid responsiveness (PLR or POCUS), check ScvO2 (target >70%), confirm source control, optimize vasopressors.\n→ CCRN KEY: MAP >=65 + UO >=0.5 mL/kg/hr do NOT guarantee adequate perfusion. Lactate non-clearance at 6h doubles 28-day mortality independent of other endpoints in septic shock.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: ScvO2 &gt;80% + non-clearing lactate = suspect cytopathic hypoxia (mitochondrial dysfunction). Cells cannot extract delivered O2 -- rising lactate reflects cellular hypoxia, not low-flow state. More fluid or vasopressors will not correct this.", "Septic shock initial lactate 7.4, at 6h repeat 6.9. Clearance = ?..."),
    (1778808560006, "Type B lactic acidosis -- most likely hepatic clearance failure (shock liver / ischemic hepatopathy) in the post-arrest state | Check LFTs, INR, bilirubin; shock liver peaks at 24-72h post-arrest. Also: thiamine (B1) deficiency (impairs pyruvate dehydrogenase), epinephrine infusion (drives glycolysis), metformin (if not held).\n→ CCRN KEY: Elevated lactate + improving hemodynamics + adequate ScvO2 = suspect impaired hepatic clearance, not ongoing tissue hypoperfusion. The liver clears ~70% of circulating lactate.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: Thiamine 200 mg IV should be given empirically in any post-arrest or septic patient with non-clearing lactate -- it is the essential cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase (pyruvate -&gt; acetyl-CoA instead of lactate). Safe, inexpensive, and effects are seen within minutes.", "Post-arrest patient: MAP 72, ScvO2 78%, adequate UO, lactate 8.1 no clearance 8h..."),
    (1778808560009, "~300-330 mL/min/m2 | VO2 plateaus at ~200-220 mL/min/m2 regardless of further DO2 increases (supply-independent region) | Below critical DO2: anaerobic metabolism begins, lactate rises, organ dysfunction develops.\n→ CCRN KEY: DO2 = CI x CaO2 x 10. Optimize all three components: Hgb/SaO2 (CaO2), cardiac output (CI), and arterial oxygen content. Targeting ScvO2 &gt;70% is a surrogate for adequate supply-demand balance above critical DO2.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: The inflection point (critical DO2) varies by patient and metabolic state -- fever, shivering, and pain increase VO2 demand and shift the critical threshold higher. Temperature management and analgesia reduce VO2 demand in critically ill patients.", "DO2/VO2 curve: O2 consumption becomes supply-dependent below critical DO2 of ~___..."),
    (1778808560012, "Pathological supply dependency | Microvascular shunting + mitochondrial dysfunction (cytopathic hypoxia) -- cells cannot extract or utilize delivered O2 | ScvO2 may be falsely elevated (>75-80%) -- venous blood returns O2-rich because cells cannot use it, NOT because delivery is excessive.\n→ CCRN KEY: High ScvO2 in septic shock does NOT reliably indicate adequate resuscitation. Use lactate trajectory, base deficit, and clinical perfusion markers alongside ScvO2. O2 extraction ratio (OER = VO2/DO2) is normally 25-30%; in cytopathic hypoxia OER may be &lt;20% despite cellular hypoxia.\n→ MASTERY NOTE: The sepsis curve in the chart stays supply-dependent across the entire DO2 range -- no plateau forms. This is pathognomonic of distributive shock with extraction failure, not simple low-output states where increasing CO or Hgb would restore the plateau.", "In sepsis, DO2/VO2 curve shows ___, meaning VO2 continues rising..."),
    (1778808560015, "Pathological O2 supply dependency with cytopathic hypoxia -- adequate delivery, impaired cellular utilization | Priority: rule out and drain undrained infectious source (definitive source control), optimize antibiotic timing and spectrum, thiamine IV empirically, avoid vasopressor escalation as the sole response to rising lactate when CI is already adequate.\n→ CCRN KEY: Do NOT interpret ScvO2 &gt;80% as 'all is well' when lactate is rising. Dysregulated O2 extraction is a defining feature of late septic shock and does not respond to more fluid or vasopressors -- treat the underlying cause (antibiotics + source control).\n→ MASTERY NOTE: Lactate &gt;4 mmol/L + ScvO2 &gt;80% + CI &gt;4 L/min/m2 = cytopathic hypoxia triad until proven otherwise. This pattern carries higher mortality than low-DO2 shock. Organ support (renal replacement, ventilator optimization) and metabolic resuscitation (thiamine, ascorbic acid) are the relevant levers -- not further DO2 augmentation.", "Septic patient: CI 4.8, Hgb 10, SaO2 98%, ScvO2 84%, lactate 6.2 rising..."),
